window.__hvBdRegister("dam-principles", {"key":"dam-principles","sections":[{"i":0,"j":9,"loc":"Chapters 1–2 (pp. 1–8)","title":"Is the principle of all beyond all things?","summary":"Damascius opens by asking whether the single principle of all things lies beyond all things or is one of them. Both answers run into trouble: if \"all things\" leaves nothing out, the principle is counted among them and stops being their principle; yet everything must be either a principle or from one. He suggests that the One is all things by having swallowed them into its simplicity, and distinguishes three ways of being all: unitarily, unifiedly and in plural fashion. Chapter 2 holds that the soul divines a principle beyond even the One, which must not be called principle, cause or first. The section closes on the objection that nothing could lie beyond the One, since the One alone causes the many.","ideas":["The principle seems both inside and outside the totality of all things","The One is not a minimum but all things in perfect simplicity","\"All\" is said unitarily, unifiedly and in plural fashion","The soul divines something beyond the One, uncoordinated with all things"],"terms":[{"t":"syntaxis (coordination)","d":"The relation that ranks a principle together with what comes from it, making them one series."},{"t":"to hen (the One)","d":"The simplest and most comprehensive principle, all things before their division."},{"t":"to hēnōmenon (the Unified)","d":"The undifferentiated concentration of the many, standing between the One and differentiated things."}]},{"i":10,"j":14,"loc":"Chapters 3–4 (pp. 8–10)","title":"Against resting in the One; the twofold nothing","summary":"Damascius answers someone who would stop at the One because nothing simpler can be conceived. He grants that the region is trackless, but argues by analogy: the relationless is more honorable than the related, as the contemplative than the statesman, so the highest must stand outside every relation, even that of first and second. Since the One and the Unified are each in some way all things, neither can be the absolute first. If what lies beyond is \"nothing,\" the nothing is twofold, one better than the One and one beneath it. All things proceed from the ineffable as from an inner sanctuary, and our words about it only report the birth-pangs of the soul standing in its forecourt.","ideas":["The uncoordinated ranks above the coordinated","What escapes every conception is most honorable","Two nothings: one above the One, one below being","Our statements report only our own birth-pangs, not the ineffable itself"],"terms":[{"t":"ōdis (birth-pang)","d":"The soul's labor toward a truth it cannot bring to utterance."},{"t":"aporrhēton (the ineffable)","d":"That which lies beyond the One and admits no statement at all."}]},{"i":15,"j":24,"loc":"Chapters 5–6 (pp. 11–17)","title":"Plato's silence and the sense of unknowability","summary":"Damascius notes that Plato already shows the One to be unknowable and unsayable in the Parmenides, and suggests that Plato led up through the One to what lies beyond it, then kept silent in the ancient manner. Demonstrations, being divided and discursive, fit neither Being nor the Forms, still less the One; the ineffable is to be honored with complete silence. Chapter 6 asks how we can write about what is wholly unknowable. Using the image of a man born blind who says color is not warm, Damascius argues that calling it unknowable reports nothing of it, only our own ignorance. Terms like \"beyond all\" or \"supra-causal\" are not its nature but removals of what comes after it.","ideas":["Plato climbs through the One to what is beyond it, then falls silent","The One is sayable in one way and unsayable in another","Calling it unknowable confesses our state, as with the blind man and color","Negations are removals, not descriptions of a nature"],"terms":[{"t":"aphairesis (removal)","d":"The stripping away of predicates, which here describes only what the first is not."},{"t":"sterēsis (privation)","d":"The absence of a form; ignorance is a privation in the knower, not a darkness in the thing."}]},{"i":25,"j":34,"loc":"Chapter 7 (pp. 17–23)","title":"The discourse overturns itself into silence","summary":"Damascius asks whether the ineffable is demonstrable or opinable, given that we say things about it. What we demonstrate is only our own ignorance, and our opinion of it is empty, like opinions about the void. He offers three arguments that it is unknowable: what is above knowledge is more honorable; anything knowable would share knowability and so be ranked among all things; and the unknowable according to the better must exist before the knowable. It is unknowable even to divine knowledge, which reaches only the One, and even the One may not know itself. The chapter ends with the claim that even negation, transcendence and the word \"ineffable\" fail, so the only limit of the discourse is silence.","ideas":["We demonstrate our ignorance about it, not something of it","Three arguments that the first is wholly unknowable","Even divine knowledge reaches only as far as the One","Even \"transcendent\" and negation must be denied of it"],"terms":[{"t":"peritropē (self-overturning)","d":"The way statements about the first refute themselves as soon as they are made."},{"t":"kenembatein (treading on emptiness)","d":"Damascius's word for thought stepping where it finds no footing."}]},{"i":35,"j":42,"loc":"Chapter 8 (pp. 24–29)","title":"Does anything come from the ineffable?","summary":"Damascius raises the puzzle that, since a monad heads its own series everywhere, there might be one ineffable and many ineffables. He replies that the ineffable has nothing in common with things here and is neither one nor many, neither cause nor causeless. He separates it from absolute non-being, which is said to be ineffable according to the worse, the first according to the better. He then argues both ways on whether it gives anything to its products: each thing seems to carry a faint trace of it, yet a participated ineffable would bring procession and order into it. The chapter closes by announcing a methodical ascent from things perceived, resting on an axiom.","ideas":["No series of ineffables, since it shares nothing with things here","Ineffable \"according to the better\" versus \"according to the worse\"","Arguments for and against a trace of the ineffable in all things","A planned ascent from the perceptible to the first"],"terms":[{"t":"henad","d":"A divine unity; each god is primarily a one heading its own series."},{"t":"proodos (procession)","d":"The going-forth of effects from a cause, which would bring rank and order into the first."}]},{"i":43,"j":51,"loc":"Chapters 9–11 (pp. 29–34)","title":"Body, Nature and irrational soul are not first","summary":"Damascius lays down the axiom of the ascent: the needless is prior to the needy. Qualified body cannot be the principle, since it is made of body and quality that need each other, and body is divisible and formless. Neither quality nor their compound is first, since each needs the other and something to gather them. Nature, the inner principle of motion and rest, is ruled out because it exists only in the bodies it shapes. The irrational soul is ruled out as well: it never turns back on itself, and its impulses are kneaded together with the body, like the cutting power of an axe.","ideas":["Axiom: what needs nothing comes before what is needy","Body and quality need each other, so neither is first","Nature needs the bodies it crafts","Irrational soul cannot turn back on itself and is bound to the body"],"terms":[{"t":"anendees (needless)","d":"Lacking nothing; the mark used to test candidates for first principle."},{"t":"physis (Nature)","d":"A principle of motion and rest within the thing moved, inseparable from it."}]},{"i":52,"j":60,"loc":"Chapters 12–13 (pp. 34–41)","title":"Rational soul, Intellect, One-Being and the One","summary":"The rational soul turns back on itself and is separable, but it does not exercise all its activities at once, so it is needy. Before it stands Intellect, unchanging, the principle Aristotle stopped at; yet Intellect is one and many, thinker and thought, so it needs its own parts. Chapter 13 tries the One-Being, which Parmenides took as principle, but following Plato it only participates in the One and needs its elements. The One itself seems wholly needless and so the principle of all. Damascius then finds even in it a topmost trace of neediness, since being principle, cause and good relates it to what comes after. The argument therefore points to something of which not even \"needless\" can be said.","ideas":["Rational soul is deficient in its changing activities","Intellect needs its own plenitudes and its intelligible object","One-Being is mixed and participates in the One","The One as principle has a topmost trace of neediness"],"terms":[{"t":"hen on (One-Being)","d":"The simplest being, the Unified, which has undergone the One rather than being it."},{"t":"to mikton (the Mixed)","d":"Being as compounded of elements, after Plato's Philebus."}]},{"i":61,"j":69,"loc":"Chapters 14–16 (pp. 41–48)","title":"Second ascent: potency before act, self-motion","summary":"Damascius begins a second way of ascent: what needs the better comes after the better, as potency comes after act. Matter comes after enmattered form, qualityless body after substantial quality, and the qualities after Nature and the vegetative power. The self-moving animal ranks above the plant, but it only seems self-moved, so the truly self-moved soul must come before it; this soul is rational or irrational. He asks whether the irrational soul moves itself and notes Syrianus's looser reading of \"self-moved\" in Plato. Chapter 16 answers that apparent self-motion belongs to the composite animal, body made perceptive or perception embodied, not to soul moving body as a separate mover.","ideas":["What needs the better ranks below it: potency below act","Substantial and accidental quality distinguished","The animal is only an apparent self-mover","Perception and the sense-organ act as one composite"],"terms":[{"t":"autokinēton (self-moved)","d":"What moves itself; strictly, only what moves and is moved as a partless whole."},{"t":"deutera hylē (second matter)","d":"Qualityless body serving as substrate for qualities."}]},{"i":70,"j":77,"loc":"Chapters 17–19 (pp. 48–52)","title":"Kinds of motion and life; the unmoved","summary":"Damascius distinguishes the motion \"by which\" a thing is moved, imparted from outside, from the motion \"according to which\" it is moved, an affection within it; otherwise the series would run to infinity. Life is twofold in the same way: the life that gives life and the life by which the ensouled lives. Self-motion in the strict sense belongs only to what turns back on itself, so beasts are self-moved only in a looser sense. Separability comes in degrees, from quality to Nature, vegetative soul, irrational soul and rational form. Chapter 19 argues that the mover comes before the moved, so an unmoved mover must stand before the self-moved soul.","ideas":["\"By which\" versus \"according to which\" in motion and life","Beasts act outward along a straight line, not in a circle on themselves","A scale of separability from quality to rational form","Unmoved mover, self-moved, moved only: the order of rank"],"terms":[{"t":"chōriston (separable)","d":"Able to subsist apart from a substrate; it comes in degrees."},{"t":"akinēton (the unmoved)","d":"What moves while remaining, and is the source of the remaining of self-moved things."}]},{"i":78,"j":83,"loc":"Chapters 20–22 (pp. 52–55)","title":"Intellect, Being, the One, and beyond","summary":"The unmoved holds unmovedly all that the self-moved soul holds, its substantial, vital and cognitive aspects, so it is one and many: Intellect. Since the unified in Intellect is mixed with difference, the purely Unified, called Being, must come before it. Chapter 21 asks whether Being is the principle and argues that the One comes before Being, since Being is unified by sharing in the One and nothing is simpler than the One. Chapter 22 shows how Plato's negations deny everything of the One except \"one\" itself. Because the One still carries the appearance of contraries, the wholly unsayable must lie before it, which completes the way through the properties.","ideas":["Intellect is the unmoved, at once unified and differentiated","Before Intellect stands the Unified, called Being","The One precedes Being; nothing simpler can be conceived","Plato never denies \"one\" of the One; the unsayable lies before it"],"terms":[{"t":"nous (Intellect)","d":"The unmoved plenitude of forms, one and many together."},{"t":"to on (Being)","d":"The Unified, holding the many in a concentration before they are divided."}]},{"i":84,"j":91,"loc":"Chapters 23–24 (pp. 56–59)","title":"Ascent through the all-perfect cosmos","summary":"Damascius begins a new method that starts from the cosmos, perfect and made of perfect parts. The cosmos must have everything found in us in a higher mode: circular nature, a life that holds it together, a heavenly imagination and appetite, and a rational self-moved soul. Its unchanging order over so long a time requires an unmoved cause beyond the self-moved soul. Chapter 24 goes from the World-Soul to an unmoved cosmos and then to a concentrated, hidden cosmos. From there the discourse rises to the all-great One, which contains all things in its simplicity, and to the Ineffable, surmised as one ineffable comprehension of all. He ends by begging the gods' pardon for this daring.","ideas":["The cosmos contains our faculties in a higher, god-fitting form","Its unvarying circuit needs an unmoved cause","Unmoved and hidden cosmos before the self-moved one","The all-great One and the Ineffable as comprehension of all"],"terms":[{"t":"pantelēs (all-perfect)","d":"Complete in every respect; the mark of the second method of ascent."},{"t":"kosmos kryphios (hidden cosmos)","d":"The undifferentiated, unified world before the visible and differentiated one."}]},{"i":92,"j":100,"loc":"Chapter 25 (pp. 59–64)","title":"Arguments that the One is knowable","summary":"Damascius sets the ineffable aside and asks whether the One itself is knowable. He gathers arguments for knowledge: we analyze up to the One and conceive it against the many; we can gather all \"ones\" into one, as infinite points into one point; anything conceived either shares the one or is lost in the infinite. Knowledge may reach the One through bastard reasoning, analogy and negation, while gods touch it by direct intuition with the flower of unitary knowledge. Plato calls the Good the greatest study, and a unitary knower implies a unitary knowable. Since the One is all things, as Linus and Pythagoras said, the knowable too seems contained in it.","ideas":["Gathering all ones into one, like many points into one point","Bastard reasoning, analogy and negation as ways of knowing","Divine unitary knowledge touches the One directly","The One as all things must include the knowable"],"terms":[{"t":"nothos logismos (bastard reasoning)","d":"Indirect inference by which matter and non-being are grasped."},{"t":"enthousiasmoi (divine possessions)","d":"Inspired states taken as evidence of a unitary, non-discursive knowing."}]},{"i":101,"j":110,"loc":"Chapters 26–27 (pp. 64–69)","title":"Counter-arguments: union beyond knowledge","summary":"Damascius answers the case for knowledge. If the One is all things, it contains the unknowable as much as the knowable, and if it knew, it would be doubled. The arguments from concentration and analogy reach only the one seated above being, not the simply One, and Plato's Parmenides denies it knowledge. Chapter 27 adds that knowledge is a reversion, and union comes before every cognitive, vital or substantial reversion, so what returns to the One does so as one to one. It even questions whether anything proceeds from or reverts to the One, since differentiation from it would make it differentiated too. The chapter ends by asking who can judge between such opposed arguments.","ideas":["The One would contain the unknowable as well as the knowable","Analogy reaches only the supersubstantial one, not the simply One","Union is prior to knowledge as the first reversion","Nothing proceeds from the One, so nothing reverts to it"],"terms":[{"t":"epistrophē (reversion)","d":"The return of an effect to its cause, by knowledge, life or being."},{"t":"henōsis (union)","d":"Conjunction of one with one, prior to and above knowledge."}]},{"i":111,"j":114,"loc":"Chapter 28 (pp. 69–75)","title":"The not-one rooted in the One","summary":"Damascius begins his own resolution. Things after the One are differentiated from it as not-one, yet their not-one is rooted in the One, which anticipates their differentiation by its union. Differentiation is not reciprocal: like the sun present to an eye blind with glaucoma, the One is present to what has stood away from it, though they are not present to it. Things can heal their separation by reversion, and the One, the same for all, is named from each participant as substantial, vital or intellective one. The One is all things before all things, the truer man, soul and sun of each, though our divided thinking loses either its simplicity or its fullness when we try to grasp it.","ideas":["The not-one is a position, not a negation, and is rooted in the One","Differentiation without reciprocity: the sun and the blind eye","The One is named from its participants but is undivided","We can hold its simplicity or its all-ness, not both at once"],"terms":[{"t":"ou-hen (not-one)","d":"What is beside the one, differentiated from it yet still one by participation."},{"t":"huparxis (existence)","d":"The bare subsistence by which the One is all things before differentiation."}]},{"i":115,"j":119,"loc":"Chapter 29 (pp. 75–80)","title":"The sudden light and the birth-pang of knowledge","summary":"Gathering divided conceptions together, we feel a trace of undivided intellection, a light kindled suddenly like fire from rubbed sticks, as radii converge on an unseen center. We rise to Being and then to the One by gathering and then letting go. From afar the One appears knowable, but near it knowledge dissolves into unknowing, as Plato's sun is seen less the closer one comes. The One lies between the knowable and the wholly unsayable, overshadowed by the ineffable's nearness. What we have is a cognitive birth-pang, which Proclus called the ineffable axiom; Damascius tells the reader to drop the \"what\" and \"what-sort\" when seeking it.","ideas":["Sudden light from mustering divided conceptions","Known from afar, unknown close at hand, like the sun","The One as all-things-one, between knowable and unsayable","Remove the \"what\" and \"what-sort\" to approach it"],"terms":[{"t":"aporrhētos axiōma (ineffable axiom)","d":"Proclus's name for the conception that labors toward the One without articulating it."},{"t":"to ti kai to poion (the what and the what-sort)","d":"The division in our thinking that Plato's Letters blame for hindering knowledge of the first."}]},{"i":120,"j":130,"loc":"Chapters 30–32 (pp. 80–85)","title":"How and how equally the One is all things","summary":"Damascius asks how the One is all things, whether equally, and how this differs from the Unified being all things. The Unified, the mixture shining forth in the third god, is all things unifiedly, while multitude is all things as many, and the One is all things before multitude and more than they are. All things are in the One equally, even more than equally, without order, since order belongs to differentiation; we name it from the terms most like it, such as the Good or the first. Chapter 32 suggests that saying \"the One is all things\" guards against ranking it as one among all; the Pythagoreans call the principle half of the whole, or more than the whole. Lines folded in a center picture how all things are simplified in it.","ideas":["Three ways of being all: One, multitude, Unified","All things are in the One equally, without order","We name the One from what most resembles it","The principle as half the whole, the whole, or more than the whole"],"terms":[{"t":"tritos theos (third god)","d":"The mixture of one and many in which Being first shines forth."},{"t":"kentron (center)","d":"Image of the One, in which all radii and the circle are folded together."}]},{"i":131,"j":137,"loc":"Chapter 33 (pp. 85–89)","title":"The One as principle and as Good","summary":"Damascius distinguishes the conception of the One that first appears to us from the determinate one paired with the many. Our conceptions bring us to the One and the Good as principle, yet these seem to be forms within oppositions such as one and many. He argues that the one by existence stands before the many as cause, not as a coordinate term, and that the supreme cause is all-making rather than merely one-making. Reading the Philebus, he says Socrates left the cause of the mixture hidden and set forward only three monads in its forecourt: truth, beauty and symmetry. The chapter concludes that we have no complete conception of it, so it may be called \"one\" no more than \"all things.\"","ideas":["The determinate one belongs to the opposition one/many","The first cause is all-making, not just one-making","Truth, beauty and symmetry as the forecourt of the Good in the Philebus","No complete conception of the first is available"],"terms":[{"t":"pantopoion (all-making)","d":"Cause of multitude, goodness and wholeness alike, and so not merely of unity."},{"t":"treis monades (three monads)","d":"Truth, beauty and symmetry, the Philebus's tokens of the hidden Good."}]},{"i":138,"j":146,"loc":"Chapters 34–35 (pp. 89–97)","title":"Whether the One proceeds into its products","summary":"Damascius argues both sides on whether anything passes from the One to what comes after it. For: things must share their cause's nature to revert and survive, Plato speaks of truth as a light from the Good, and each thing has a one before its many, the root of its subsistence. Against: procession requires differentiation, which belongs to multitude, and even Parmenides' Being does not proceed; perishable things also could not have a perishable trace of the One. Chapter 35 proposes that all things share one undivided participation, as in sunlight, with the difference lying in the participants. The One gives only itself, like a root that is already the tree, while a separate multitude-making cause accounts for processions.","ideas":["Arguments for a trace of the One in all things","Arguments that the One cannot proceed or impart anything","One undivided participation, with the difference in the participants","A multitude-making cause distinct from the One"],"terms":[{"t":"methexis (participation)","d":"Sharing in a cause; here one and the same for all participants."},{"t":"plēthopoion aition (multitude-making cause)","d":"The cause of differentiation and procession, set apart from the One."}]},{"i":147,"j":152,"loc":"Chapter 36","title":"The all-making cause beyond cause and effect","summary":"Damascius restarts the inquiry into the One that is all things in the simplest manner. It is neither unifying nor multiplying, gives no proper participation of itself, and is neither participated nor unparticipated; even matter, the last dregs, is held within its undifferenced simplicity. It is called cause of all only by borrowing from lower things, since producing, potency and activity are all determinations. He then asks how what comes after it is differentiated from it, using the analogies of sun and moon, form and matter, and image and paradigm, and ends in perplexity over how what is beyond all determination can be set apart from anything.","ideas":["The One-all is neither one thing nor many but all things undeterminedly","It is cause of all without being a determined productive, paradigmatic or final cause","Its procession is neither uniform nor non-uniform but before both","Things that proceed set themselves apart from it; it does not set itself apart from them"],"terms":[{"t":"diorismos (determination)","d":"The marking-off of a thing as this rather than that, which Damascius denies of the first principle."},{"t":"one-all (hen panta)","d":"His name for the first principle as all things in one simple manner."}]},{"i":153,"j":162,"loc":"Chapters 37–39","title":"The birth-pang, ascent with all, unitary procession","summary":"Damascius gives an indication of how anything can come after That: our speech about it is a birth-pang that labours but never gives birth, and the second is best pictured as a potency welded to existence. All things, first to last, came forth together and stand toward the one-all as effects toward a cause, while order among them comes from other causes. He cites Iamblichus that no single thing ascends to That except together with all things. Things neither proceeded nor did not proceed; That neither gives nor withholds participation, and we say it produces only through the weakness of our thought.","ideas":["Talk of the first is an ever-labouring birth-pang that never delivers","All things proceed together and ascend together (Iamblichus)","The first is before existence, potency and activity, so before 'producing'","Oppositions such as union and differentiation do not apply to it"],"terms":[{"t":"ōdis (birth-pang)","d":"Damascius's image for the soul's straining toward a conception of the first that it can never bring forth."}]},{"i":163,"j":168,"loc":"Chapters 40–41","title":"Light of truth, henads, and dishonoring conceptions","summary":"Damascius asks what Plato's light of truth sent forth from the Good is, and says Plato there means the principle on which Being hangs, not the One before all. If truth is called its light, it is the procession of the henads, whose common root proceeds together with them and is a participation of That, not That itself. The first is neither root nor branches but all together before both, beyond every opposition, even contradiction. On the radiance of the soul, he concludes that the only knowledge of That is to dishonor every conception we have of it, including cause, first, Good and beyond-being.","ideas":["The light of truth belongs to the principle just above Being","The single root of the gods proceeds with its branches and is not the first","The first is neither perishable nor imperishable, beyond all opposition","We know That only by rejecting all our conceptions as unfit"],"terms":[{"t":"henads","d":"The divine unities whose procession may be called the light of the first."}]},{"i":169,"j":179,"loc":"Chapters 42–45","title":"Remaining, procession, and two principles before the triad","summary":"Damascius asks how the second relates to the first and denies that it is either differentiated from it or unified with it; remaining, procession and reversion do not yet apply. By analogy only, That may answer to remaining, the first from it to procession, and the third to reversion, with the one-all serving as the remaining to halt an infinite regress. He then asks whether there are two principles before the first intelligible triad, the ineffable and an uncoordinated one, as Iamblichus held in his Chaldaean Theology, or whether Porphyry's Father of the triad is the first. He leans toward Iamblichus, citing the Pythagorean one before monad and dyad, Plato's Philebus, and the Oracle of a triad ruled by a monad, and concludes that after the all-things-one come principles like limit and unlimited or father and power.","ideas":["Same and other, union and differentiation are not yet present at the top","Remaining, procession, reversion apply to the first principles only by analogy","Iamblichus posits a one between the Ineffable and the intelligible triad","After the one-all come a pair: limit and unlimited, monad and dyad, father and power"],"terms":[{"t":"monē, proodos, epistrophē (remaining, procession, reversion)","d":"The three moments of Neoplatonic causation, which Damascius applies to the first principles only by analogy."},{"t":"intelligible triad","d":"The first triad of the intelligible order, Father, Power and Intellect in Chaldaean terms."}]},{"i":180,"j":183,"loc":"Chapter 46","title":"The two columns argument and Damascius's doubts","summary":"Damascius states an argument from below: beings fall into two columns, one-like and multitude-like, which point to two principles and to a single common principle above them. Asked whether to follow Iamblichus, who alone posited two principles beyond the intelligible triads, he says these arguments do not seem firm. Carrying determined names like limit and unlimited up to the principles would multiply principles and introduce opposition there. Names drawn from determined things should lead us up by analogy, not be deposited in the principles, as the analogy of sight and hearing for intellect and soul shows.","ideas":["The two columns of opposites suggest two principles and a one above them","Damascius rejects the argument as not firm","Transferring determinate names would make many principles There","Different schools' names are indications of one nature, not literal descriptions"],"terms":[{"t":"systoichia (column)","d":"One of the two Pythagorean series of opposites, here one-like and multitude-like."}]},{"i":184,"j":190,"loc":"Chapters 47–48","title":"Symbols of one nature; paternal and maternal principles","summary":"Damascius treats monad, limit, father, existence and aether as symbols of one nature, and unlimited, dyad, power and chaos as symbols of the second. The Egyptians called the ineffable a thrice-proclaimed unknowable darkness; Pythagoras's one before the monad and Plato's one of the first hypothesis of the Parmenides are symbols of it, while the second hypothesis posits the one-all. He then argues that the one-all heads the one-like column and multitude the other, and that the one does not proceed but remains, while all procession is dyadic. He accepts three, the Ineffable, the one-like and the multitude-like, provided they are not opposed as equals, and ends calling the two principles the paternal and maternal causes of the unified.","ideas":["Names of the schools are symbols of the same principles","The Egyptians' unknowable darkness indicates the Ineffable","The one is without procession; procession begins with the dyad","Each of the two principles rules both columns, as father and mother of the unified"],"terms":[{"t":"unknowable darkness","d":"The Egyptian name for the ineffable principle that Damascius cites."},{"t":"one-all","d":"The second principle after the Ineffable, posited affirmatively, as in the Parmenides' second hypothesis."}]},{"i":191,"j":196,"loc":"Chapter 49","title":"The One, the Unified, and the not-one","summary":"Following Plato, Damascius distinguishes the One from the Unified, which has been affected by the one and is both one and not-one, so that before it stand the purely one and the purely not-one, called unlimited, chaos, dyad or multitude. Being is thus mixed, and the first pair of principles is the One and the Many, of which other names are indications. The one does not proceed, so multitude must begin procession and differentiation. He sets out the case for a single one before the two, citing the Philebus, Pythagoras and the Homeric one king, then answers that the one is not wholly unsayable, being grasped by the unitary flower of the soul, and so cannot be the ineffable principle of all.","ideas":["The Unified participates the one and also has the not-one","The first pair of principles is One and Many","Multitude is the cause of procession and differentiation","The one is known by unitary intuition, so it is not the Ineffable"],"terms":[{"t":"hēnōmenon (the Unified)","d":"What has been affected by the one: Being as one and not-one together."},{"t":"flower of the soul","d":"The unitary form of knowledge by which the one may be touched, ending in union rather than knowledge."}]},{"i":197,"j":204,"loc":"Chapters 50–51","title":"The case for a dyad and Iamblichus's middle one","summary":"Damascius presents the case for a dyad after the one: Orpheus's aether and chaos after Time, the Oracles' father and power, and Plato's and Philolaus's Being from limit and unlimited. A defender of Iamblichus argues from the two channels of opposites to two principles with a single summit above them, a middle one between the two and the wholly unsayable. Damascius replies that our conceptions of one and all are purified together, and asks what the one cause of the two channels' connate growth is. If it is connate it is not simply one, and if it is simply one it does not explain the connate growth, so the middle principles would be two, not one as Iamblichus holds.","ideas":["Theologies and philosophers alike posit two after the one","Iamblichus's defender places a simply one between the Ineffable and the two","A cause of connate growth cannot also be the simply one","The objection multiplies middle principles"],"terms":[{"t":"symphyia (connate growth)","d":"The growing-together of the two opposed series, whose cause is in question."}]},{"i":205,"j":214,"loc":"Chapters 52–53","title":"Refuting the middle one; the three principles named","summary":"Damascius answers: the ascent should go from all things, not from one counter-division; the counter-division does not include the partless procession before it; Pythagoras's one only covertly indicates the Ineffable, which the Egyptians called darkness beyond all intellection; such channels regress infinitely; and all division belongs to the multitude-making dyad. Counter-division actually begins in the third intelligible order, the Fount of founts. Fifth, he ascends from all partition to the union-mass of all, which is Being, the Unified, and above it to the many alone and the one alone. He ends by naming the three: the One before all, All things, and the One-all according to union, comparing the third to the Orphic Metis, fount of the fontal orders.","ideas":["Ascent must start from all things, not one opposition","Every division is the offspring of multitude","The second principle is pure manyness, the unlimited by herself","The three are the One, the All, and the Unified"],"terms":[{"t":"Metis","d":"The Orphic figure bearing the seed of gods, called fount of the fontal world-orders."},{"t":"union-mass","d":"Damascius's term for the third principle as all things gathered undividedly."}]},{"i":215,"j":224,"loc":"Chapters 54–55","title":"Neither two nor one; why the third is Mixed","summary":"Damascius says the first two principles are not two in number, not of equal rank and not set apart by otherness; we misuse names for them, and per Iamblichus even intellect must fold its intellections together to touch the intelligible. The first is one-all, the second all-one, and the third the Unified or Being. He then turns to the third principle, called Mixed by Plato, Philolaus and the Pythagoreans, and by Orpheus figured as the silvery egg fashioned by Chronos. He asks why the Unified is not directly after the One and from what it is mixed, rejects making the two principles its elements, and suggests that their participations act on one another in the third, producing union and differentiation from which the Mixed is formed.","ideas":["Dyad and number do not yet exist among the first principles","Our names for them are indications that do not hit the truth","The third is Mixed, like the Orphic egg","Union and differentiation arise in the third from the two participations"],"terms":[{"t":"mikton (the Mixed)","d":"Plato's Philebus term for what comes of limit and unlimited, applied to the third principle."}]},{"i":225,"j":230,"loc":"Chapter 56","title":"The Mixed without elements, parts, or wholeness","summary":"Damascius considers how the two principles produce the Mixed and its elements, and whether it is a both-together of the one and the many; this threatens an infinite regress of wholes. He distinguishes elements, parts and forms, placing forms in the third intelligible order, parts in the middle and elements at the summit. The first Mixed has no differentiated elements; its multitude is like one water, much-divisible, hidden under its union. It is one simple property, formed as the outpouring of the many congealed by the one, and he closes with a prayer to the gods to forgive the weakness of such conceptions.","ideas":["The first Mixed is not composite, a whole of parts, or of elements","Forms, parts and elements belong to the three intelligible orders","Its multitude is like one water, much-divisible","The Mixed is the congealed flow of the unlimited"],"terms":[{"t":"stoicheion (element)","d":"A constituent fused within the mixed so that its property does not show, unlike parts or forms."}]},{"i":231,"j":241,"loc":"Chapters 57–58","title":"The Mixed as monad; senses of Being","summary":"Damascius restates the doctrine with Plato's limit and unlimited, whose product is the Limited, and with the Pythagorean monad and dyad, whose product is the unified triad. The Mixed is like the monad that holds all number, a monad by indication, and its parents may be called father and mother of one first-born. He then distinguishes four senses of Being in Plato: a genus, the plenitude of genera, the whole before soul, and the summit of the intelligible. The third principle gathers both the simple genus and the all-full substance, and lacks any proper name, as the mixture of our body's four elements is nameless, though they subsist humanly, horsely, lunarly or solarly under a form.","ideas":["The Mixed is like the monad containing all number undifferentiated","Being is said in four ways in Plato's dialogues","The third is called Being and Substance in both simple and full senses","Mixtures lack proper names; a form names and gathers its elements"],"terms":[{"t":"ousia (substance)","d":"Used for the third principle as the first Being, though only by indication."}]},{"i":242,"j":250,"loc":"Chapters 59–60","title":"Names from afar; how Being is constituted","summary":"Damascius compares our names to mountains and stars seen from afar, small and faint; the most general properties reach us while the rest are lost. The summit is called Being not from one property but because it is all things, a root never leaving the one, and he cites Iamblichus that the intelligible subsists in and about the one. He explains that the first fled into the simplicity of the one and the second into the void of multitude, while the third, bound by the one, became Being. He separates a unitary and a substantial Unified, and calls the monad Titanic. Invoking god, he concludes that the Unified is a single mean between one and many, citing Parmenides that being draws nigh to being.","ideas":["Our names are narrowed images of distant plenitudes","Being arises where the many are twisted together by the one","The unified is double: unitary and substantial","The Unified is a mean, not a composite"],"terms":[{"t":"Titanic monad","d":"The monad as already isolated toward determination, unlike the several within the Unified."}]},{"i":251,"j":259,"loc":"Chapters 61–62","title":"Existence, power, activity; what Being means","summary":"Damascius argues that the first principle answers to existence, the second to power, and the third to activity, so the third is substance or Being. He discusses Iamblichus's distinction of existing and being, and says the first activity is reversion, found in the intelligible intellect; the third is thus also the first intellect of the Chaldaean triad, substantial intellect, as Amelius may call it. He then asks what Being means: what gives each thing its being, a bond of the forms as 'is' binds speech. He criticizes the Sophist's definition by power, Strato's view that Being is remaining, and etymologies of existing, subsisting and related verbs.","ideas":["Existence, power and activity map onto the three principles","The first activity is reversion toward the cause","Being is the root and bond of all forms","Remaining and subsistence do not define Being"],"terms":[{"t":"hyparxis (existence)","d":"The bare subsistence-making character, indicating the first principle."},{"t":"dynamis (power)","d":"The first stretching-out of existence, indicating the second principle."}]},{"i":260,"j":265,"loc":"Chapters 63–64","title":"Being from going; activity is not motion","summary":"Damascius takes up the Cratylus etymology of Being from going, so that Being is what is able to act, and reports a dream saying Being is the in-actuality of each thing. He argues that motion is one genus opposed to rest, not the same as power or activity, since rest also acts, and the genera share in one another by an exchange of friendship. Against philosophers who equate activity with motion, he argues that suffering can be a work, that the dancing is the dance, and that each thing's activity is that thing acting. He ends asking whether activity is a form of its own or each thing's stretching-out.","ideas":["Being as the in-actuality of each thing","Motion is a genus counter-divided against Being, not its stretching-out","Activity is the rendering of a work, not motion","Every form has an activity of its own kind"],"terms":[{"t":"energeia (activity)","d":"The fulfillment of a thing's proper work, which Damascius distinguishes from kinēsis (motion)."}]},{"i":266,"j":274,"loc":"Chapters 65–66","title":"Power, the first Mixed, and the One Being","summary":"Damascius raises the same questions about power and holds that existence, power and activity are folded together and undifferentiated in the first Mixed. The activity of Being is its substance, and the genus Being is the most unified of the genera, so it indicates the unified summit, as the one indicates the first. He defines Being as what each thing is in actuality, with the three still one at the summit. Finally he says the summit is neither Being nor substance nor one, but the unified as a mean between one and Being, which Plato in the Parmenides called the One Being, as he called soul a mean between partless and partitioned substance.","ideas":["In the summit existence, power and activity are one","The genus Being best indicates the unified principle","We do not partition That; we are partitioned about it","Plato's One Being names a single middle nature"],"terms":[{"t":"One Being (hen on)","d":"Plato's phrase in the Parmenides, read as naming the mean between the one and Being."}]},{"i":275,"j":286,"loc":"Chapters 67–69 (pp. 185–192)","title":"The unified as One Being, mean of one and being","summary":"Damascius asks why the unified is called Being and intelligible when it is all things undifferentiatedly, and enlists Iamblichus, for whom the intelligible stays about the one, and Plato's division of the One Being in the Parmenides. He adduces the Phaedrus' plain of truth, the Cratylus, and the Orphic Night who nurses Kronos as witnesses to the intelligible. He then argues the contrary hypothesis — that after the supersubstantial comes substance, the one and being yoked without any wedge of otherness — and concludes the dispute is largely about a name. The settled view: the first is neither one nor being, the middle is One Being as a single both-together nature before blending, and the third is one and being separately, set apart by otherness, as Plato's three hypotheses show.","ideas":["The unified is counter-distinguished against nothing; it only seems to be Being or intelligible","One and Being are so connate that only with otherness does substance stand against the one as vehicle to rider","The mean is a blending before the blended, as body, soul and intellect pre-exist in the demiurge","Plato denies both to the first, gathers One Being in the middle, separates them in the third"],"terms":[{"t":"hēnōmenon (the unified)","d":"The third principle, all things undifferentiatedly, before the split into one and substance."},{"t":"One Being (hen on)","d":"The Parmenides' term for the both-together of one and being, taken here as the unified mean."},{"t":"heterotēs (otherness)","d":"The principle whose first shining-forth separates one from being and begins the intellectual order."}]},{"i":287,"j":294,"loc":"Chapters 70–71 (pp. 192–200)","title":"Is the unified knowable or unknowable?","summary":"Granting Iamblichus that the unified is graspable by no opinion, reason or intuition, Damascius sets arguments on both sides. Against knowability: the knowable is being, knowledge implies determination and reversion, and the unified neither proceeds nor reverts and cannot be circumscribed. For it: we ourselves surmise it, the divine intellect knows it, and the Oracles teach the gods' instruction to intelligize the intelligible by the flower of intellect with an empty, turned-away eye. He concludes there is a unified, intelligible knowledge that is circumscribed by its object rather than circumscribing it, poured out whole into it; the unified contains, hiddenly, a first, middle and third (substance, life, intelligible intellect), and is both most knowable and most unknowable.","ideas":["Ordinary knowledge stands at a distance from its object; the unified admits no such distance","The Chaldaean Oracle on the flower of intellect describes a knowledge that lets itself go into the intelligible","Knowledge is ninth from the first substance: three reversions within each of three ranks","Within the unified there is a hidden triad, so a knowing third need not make substance cognitive"],"terms":[{"t":"anthos nou (flower of intellect)","d":"The Oracles' faculty for intelligizing the intelligible without fixing on it as an object."},{"t":"epistrophē (reversion)","d":"The return of what has proceeded toward its source, here substantial, vital or cognitive."}]},{"i":295,"j":301,"loc":"Chapters 72–73 (pp. 200–204)","title":"How the first differentiated differs from the undifferentiated","summary":"Damascius raises the puzzle that whatever is differentiated seems to be differentiated from something itself differentiated, which would make the unified differentiated too. He answers that relatives need not convert symmetrically: form is other than matter, but matter is not other than form, since otherness is itself a form; likewise the first differentiation is the form's from the super-eidetic, which stays undifferentiated. Differentiation and sameness take on the mode of each thing in which they occur — eternal in the eternal, by cause in the cause. The unified's privilege is not to be its own but the one's; it is 'differentiated' from the differentiated only so as to remain undifferentiated, and the shared name is a concession of language.","ideas":["Cause and effect do not convert as coordinate relatives do","Matter is not other than form, because otherness is a form and matter formless","Each term has differentiation in its own manner, as image and paradigm share likeness unequally","Names belong to determined, eidetic things, so we syllogize truly but name improperly"],"terms":[{"t":"hyperousion / super-eidetic","d":"What is above form and so cannot suffer differentiation, which is form-like."}]},{"i":302,"j":307,"loc":"Chapter 74 (pp. 204–209)","title":"What it means to remain while proceeding","summary":"Damascius examines the axiom that what proceeds remains in its producer. He tests and rejects several readings: remaining as keeping the producer's common property, as having a root or cause in the producer, as the producer remaining while the product goes forth, and as one part remaining while another proceeds. Each either makes the claim trivial or tears product from producer. His answer is that the proceeded thing, whole, remains precisely in the respect in which it has proceeded, being most akin to its begetter, like a son almost the same in form as his father; our partitioning concepts must be forced to unfold toward the truth. Everything both remains and proceeds, with remaining dominating above and procession below.","ideas":["Remaining cannot mean being in the cause as in a place or vessel","Sameness is in degree: There things other in form are more united than things the same here","What in no way is its source has not proceeded from it at all","Human thought partitions the impartible and must be trained to broaden"],"terms":[{"t":"monē (remaining)","d":"The abiding of an effect in its cause, here explained as kinship and sameness with it."},{"t":"proodos (procession)","d":"The going-forth of the effect, explained as its otherness and differentiation from the cause."}]},{"i":308,"j":313,"loc":"Chapter 75 (pp. 209–214)","title":"Reversion as longing for the ancient nature","summary":"Damascius asks what reversion adds once the proceeded already remains in its source. He rejects reversion as a recovery of remaining or as gaining an image, and proposes that it is the desire of the third for the first, possible only after they stand apart: procession makes the third's substance, reversion perfects it. He raises further puzzles — two rests between two motions, whether reversion and procession are double, whether only the third has all three. His proposal: in the third, remaining (the first's nature mingled in it), procession (its standing apart) and reversion (the union of these, which gives it its form) are blended like elements, so it remains in, proceeds from and reverts to itself as monad, dyad and triad.","ideas":["Reversion belongs only to what has proceeded","Procession is substance-making, reversion perfection-making","Remaining may be double: one before procession and one before reversion","The third is constituted by the three as elements of its substance"],"terms":[{"t":"epistrophē (reversion)","d":"The proceeded's desire for and return toward its source, here also what gives it its proper character."}]},{"i":314,"j":320,"loc":"Chapters 76–77 (pp. 214–217)","title":"Toward itself and its prior; why three","summary":"Damascius holds that reversion, procession and remaining toward oneself and toward one's prior are one in subject and double only in relation, since a thing's not wholly departing from its cause is the same as its not departing from itself. The three are gathered and undifferentiated in the first, being-differentiated in the middle, and first properly determined in the third, which then relates triply both to the second and to the first. Once they appear in the first intellect they run through every intellect, with remaining dominant at the summit, procession in the middle and reversion at the end. He justifies the triad: procession shows variation, remaining shows the begetting form, and reversion presents the begotten as a determinate thing.","ideas":["One reversion makes a thing both itself and like its prior","Strictly, even remaining cannot be said of the first, except by analogy","The three participate one another, so lower remaining participates higher","Remaining and procession are contradictories; reversion adds the settling of a character"],"terms":[]},{"i":321,"j":331,"loc":"Chapters 78–80 (pp. 217–223)","title":"Substantial, vital and cognitive reversion; ten aporiai","summary":"Each of remaining, procession and reversion is divided into substantial, vital and cognitive. Damascius argues there must be something self-subsistent, a mean between what subsists from elsewhere and what does not subsist, and that self-constitution is substantial reversion. Toward Being, intellect reverts substantially by entering its own bound and so becoming akin to Being's bound, vitally by contact through life, and cognitively by knowing it from afar; it likewise reverts toward life, turned to its cause as the cause's rather than its own. He then lists ten aporiai: on the triadic division itself, whether only the third reverts, the triple remaining and procession, why only knowledge names knower, known and knowledge, how intellect knows its prior, the end of knowledge, and whether knower or known acts.","ideas":["The self-subsistent is a mean, as the self-moved is between unmoved and other-moved","Substantial reversion makes a thing like its prior; cognitive reversion only knows it","Intellect is three-formed: being, living and intelligizing","The aporiai set the agenda answered in reverse order in the following chapters"],"terms":[{"t":"authypostaton (self-subsistent)","d":"What gives itself subsistence, interpreted as reversion upon itself according to substance."}]},{"i":332,"j":336,"loc":"Chapter 81, first part (pp. 223–228)","title":"Three whole cosmoi and the origin of knowledge","summary":"Answering the last aporia first, Damascius treats substance, life and intellect not as genera but as whole cosmoi: substance gathers all undifferentiatedly, life is in travail with all things in the being-differentiated, intellect has all things differentiated and circumscribed. Priors do not participate posteriors, so neither substance nor life has knowledge. Knowledge arises only where otherness has made things stand apart; it is the consolation of that standing-apart, joining knower to known through love of truth. In intellect the cognitive reversion is the farthest joining, ninth from the undifferentiated, and he derives noēsis from a 'going' toward being.","ideas":["Life is the seething, stirred middle between uncircumscribed and circumscribed","Without otherness there is no knower, known and knowledge","Intellect's first intellection is its circumscribed return into Being, before knowledge","Knowledge does not dissolve the standing-apart but leads back across it"],"terms":[{"t":"noēsis (intellection)","d":"Etymologized as a going and return toward being."},{"t":"gnōsis (knowledge)","d":"The relation joining things that stand apart, the last reversion of the proceeded."}]},{"i":337,"j":341,"loc":"Chapter 81, second part (pp. 228–232)","title":"The shining of Being and the knowable","summary":"Damascius asks what the knowable is as distinct from Being. He proposes that knowledge attains Being as knowable, through its shining, a light escorting itself forth toward the knower, as the sensible is what a form projects toward sensation. Pressed on whether intellect then knows only a surface or an efflux, he answers that knowability is not a property in Being but appears by contrast with intellect: Being becomes knowable only because intellect, differentiated from it, turns back cognitively. Properly, knowable, knowing and knowledge are differentiated in intellect, which knows itself; toward Being its gathered knowledge becomes a contact of substance with substance. Substance is the desired and knowable, intellect the needy knower.","ideas":["Every first keeps something unsayable to its seconds","The knowable shines in Being only relative to intellect's standing-apart","Every form has some knowledge and life; even stones and earth are not wholly soulless","Toward Being intellect's return is substantial, toward itself cognitive"],"terms":[{"t":"gnōsma (the known-as-known)","d":"The known itself as it comes to exist within the knower."}]},{"i":342,"j":351,"loc":"Chapters 82–83 (pp. 232–239)","title":"Cause acting, the use of knowledge, knowing the prior","summary":"On the ninth aporia, Damascius argues with the Phaedo's examples of number and the cut rod that the caused does not act on the cause: the cause makes itself knowable and desired and breathes desire into what stood away, as Iamblichus says the intelligible fills intellect with intellection of itself. Knowledge gives the knower its being, shows the knowable in its object, and forms the caused by its causes; it guides desire toward the Good like a fore-running eye, though it joins us to Being, not to the Unsayable. On the seventh to fourth aporiai: intellect knows its prior by knowing itself as that prior's imitation, while Being lets itself be known out of love for its offspring; knowledge is a recognition across a gap, not a merging. Knowledge is not productive, and names like knower and known appear only where differentiation is notable.","ideas":["The higher grants both the power and the act of knowing it","Passionless knowledge of the worse raises it toward the better","Knowledge is a greeting stretched across the standing-apart","Life and substance have unnamed counterparts of knower, known and knowledge"],"terms":[{"t":"tender love for the offspring","d":"Damascius' image of Being making itself commensurate to intellect so as to be known."}]},{"i":352,"j":357,"loc":"Chapter 84 (pp. 239–243)","title":"Double procession; why substance, life, intellect","summary":"Damascius distinguishes the procession of the proceeded from procession in the act of proceeding, which is life, where the three cannot yet be divided. The three are fully differentiated only in the proceeded; in Being they are merely ascribed by us, since Being simply is, while life is a birth-pang and advance of them. He then justifies the triad of the first aporia: undifferentiated, being-differentiated, differentiated; one, multiplied, and mean; and substance, life, knowledge as seen in things that only are, also live, also know. To be is by itself, to know stretches toward another, to live is the seething middle; remaining, procession and reversion parallel health, falling ill, and recovery.","ideas":["Being does not properly remain, proceed or revert","Life holds the rank of power relative to substance, knowledge of activity","Intellect is third as circumscribed, differentiated and returning","The Chaldaean Power is an example of being-differentiated"],"terms":[{"t":"zōē (life)","d":"The middle of substance and intellect, its seething and up-standing."}]},{"i":358,"j":363,"loc":"Chapter 85 (pp. 243–249)","title":"Many as forms, parts and elements","summary":"To ask whether the unified has a first, middle and last within it, Damascius turns to multitude. Things are many as forms, which stand apart each wishing to be itself; as parts, which belong to one another and the whole; and as elements, which merge into a blend and show no separate circumscription. He sets out how parts and elements differ and how higher kinds participate lower ones, so that forms can also be parts or elements, as with organic parts, cosmic zones, mules and ostriches, and the genera of Being. Elements are usually simpler and prior, but not always: the first substance has no elder elements, its elements shining forth within it.","ideas":["Parts are of the same elements as the whole; elements are simpler than any part","Unlike parts are at once forms and parts","The elementated uses its elements like matter, being a form upon them","The first elementated is simpler and better than what it contains"],"terms":[{"t":"stoicheion (element)","d":"A constituent that blends into a whole and keeps no separate partition."},{"t":"homoiomerē (like-parted)","d":"Parts sharing the form and name of the whole, such as flesh."}]},{"i":364,"j":369,"loc":"Chapter 86 (pp. 249–255)","title":"The threefold one and elements of first substance","summary":"Damascius distinguishes the elementated as supervening form from the elementated as mixture, and holds that the first substance, before all determination, shines forth its own elements within itself. Every one precedes its multitude and slackens into it; so there are three ones: the eidetic monad over forms, the whole over parts, and the elementated over elements, the last most unified. Parts and whole are the middle manner, and he places whole and parts before all forms in life, the seething middle, with simple substance before it as first elementated. Its elements are the genera of Being, but stripped of eidetic determination: elements by existence, parts by participation, forms only lower down.","ideas":["The many grow out of the one without using it up","Unified elements, inclining parts, and autonomous forms are the three grades of multitude","Our names and thoughts are eidetic, at best psychic","All forms, down to the last, pre-exist gathered in the first"],"terms":[{"t":"genē tou ontos (genera of Being)","d":"Being, sameness, otherness, motion, rest, taken here as elements of the first substance."}]},{"i":370,"j":381,"loc":"Chapters 87–88 (pp. 255–265)","title":"Individuals, subaltern forms, double procession of forms","summary":"Forms nearer the unified behave as elements, the most specific as forms, and the subaltern as both. Damascius asks whether species are elements of individuals and answers that a second kind of differentiation sets the same whole form in many matters: Socrates is not other than Plato by form, only here and there, and generation is twofold, by form-making differences and by scattering of the same. He then argues that elements and parts always proceed with their one, and lower things are gathered in higher ones, all pre-existing and not pre-existing. Forms have a double procession: as parts with the whole, which agrees with Parmenides on part and whole, and as autonomous wholes after a titanic differentiation, still ordered like a chorus with its leader.","ideas":["The individual is called atom because the whole form is in it undivided","Number and otherness are forms, so individuals of one species are not other by form","Monads of a triad differ from those of a dyad, as man's elements are proper to him","Autonomous forms depend from their monad like a number or a chain"],"terms":[{"t":"atomon (individual)","d":"The particular, in which one species is set whole in a particular matter."}]},{"i":382,"j":388,"loc":"Chapter 89 (pp. 265–272)","title":"The simply many and the One Many","summary":"Damascius asks what the simply many are, before forms, parts and elements, and answers that they are the multiplication of the simply one, neither blended nor separate, only many, as an affection and inner procession of the one. He identifies them with the Parmenides' unlimited multitude and the One Many where the first hypothesis' negations begin, called by Chaldaeans fount of founts, by Orpheus the mixed bearing the seed of gods, by Phoenicians cosmic Aiōn. Since these titles are usually given to the limit of the intelligibles, he weighs whether the simply one, the first and simply god, comes after the unified, or whether One Being is double-faced, both limit of intelligibles and fount of intellectuals. He leaves this to the gods, then sets the simply one, simply many and simply substance as the first three principles before the double file of henads and substances.","ideas":["No multitude constitutes itself; it multiplies about a monad, whole or mixture","The simply one is principle of both unitary and substantial processions","Theological testimonies support each hypothesis, and the truth is left open","The unified's summit, middle and third are called mixed, whole and monad by analogy"],"terms":[{"t":"hen polla (One Many)","d":"The one together with its unlimited multitude, distinguished from One Being."},{"t":"plēthos apeiron (unlimited multitude)","d":"The Parmenides' multitude before any otherness, read as the simply many."}]},{"i":389,"j":395,"loc":"Chapter 90, pp. 272–277","title":"Does the unified proceed? Like-formed and unlike-formed procession","summary":"Damascius opens the inquiry into procession by asking whether the unified (the One Being after the One) proceeds within or from itself. He argues both sides: a nature wholly unified seems to admit no differentiation into first, middle and last, which would be like working darkness into light; yet the gods and theologians hymn intelligible triads, and substance, life and intellect do proceed from it. He then asks whether outer multitudes come from their producer like-formedly (Athena from Athena) or unlike-formedly (Athena from Zeus), and shows that each kind contains something of the other, since every god is a summit with its own peculiarity. The chapter ends by suggesting that the same thing may be called the existence of one thing and the cause of another.","ideas":["A wholly unified nature seems unable to contain any first, middle and last","Theological tradition and the argument both demand some procession in the intelligible","Like-formed procession proves also unlike-formed, and unlike-formed also like-formed","Existence of the producer and cause of the produced may be the same thing seen two ways"],"terms":[{"t":"the unified (hēnōmenon)","d":"The One Being immediately after the One, where one and being are not yet differentiated."},{"t":"like-formed / unlike-formed procession","d":"Procession in which the product shares the producer's form (Athena from Athena) or takes a different one (Athena from Zeus)."},{"t":"inner multitude","d":"The many contained within a cause, from which its outer offspring are begotten."}]},{"i":396,"j":401,"loc":"Chapter 91, pp. 277–282","title":"How many come from one: the second principle","summary":"Damascius asks how many can proceed from one, observing that even Zeus's many offspring seem to come from a many already contained in him, and that containing cosmoi (Ouranian, Kronian, Dian) always exceed what they contain. He raises the old dilemma that procession can come neither from being nor from not-being, questions what it means for the effect to pre-exist in its cause, and asks how the unified could cause differentiation, its contrary. His answer is that the One is not merely one set against many but all things by an all-bearing simplicity, and that a second principle, the many of the One, unlimited power or Paternal Power, is generative of all. The cause co-produces procession, descent and differentiation along with the things that proceed.","ideas":["Every producer partitions its offspring from a multitude within it","Procession seems impossible both from being and from not-being","The One causes all by being all things in one simplicity before all antithesis","The second principle is a loosing and unlimitedness of the One, generative of all"],"terms":[{"t":"all-bearing simplicity","d":"The One's way of being all things before all things, without multiplicity."},{"t":"Paternal Power","d":"Name by which the gods invoke the second principle, the many of the One."}]},{"i":402,"j":409,"loc":"Chapters 92–93, pp. 282–287","title":"Grades of union; images, paradigms and the four aporiai","summary":"Damascius ranks the kinds of union, from union of the same rank as differentiation up through the monad of monads and the whole to the most proper union, the One Being hymned as the unified; he warns that we name the undetermined only through determined names, and must gather our concepts into one summit. Turning to the remaining difficulties, he holds that forms such as the swollen and the dispersed are first, as forms, in intellect and only last in matter; partible and impartible name the same form in its generated and ungenerated subsistence. Producers and products are the same in one way and not in another, so being comes from being and not-being from not-being. Many from one is no wonder, since the One is all things and the second principle is all things as many.","ideas":["The unified is the most proper union, containing even differentiation by way of union","Every form is in both intellect and matter: there by existence, here by participation","Things are in the unmoved unmovedly, in the self-moved self-movedly, in the other-moved other-movedly","All things come from the One as numbered, from the second principle as still unlimited"],"terms":[{"t":"paradigm and image","d":"The ungenerated eternal form and its enmattered likeness, both forms but in first and last subsistence."},{"t":"trans-thinking","d":"Gathering all concepts into one to grasp a trace of the undetermined nature."}]},{"i":410,"j":420,"loc":"Chapters 94–95, pp. 287–293","title":"Whole cosmoi from whole cosmoi; depth and breadth","summary":"Damascius argues that when a whole cosmos proceeds from a whole (the demiurgic from the titanic), nothing sterile remains above: all things are in every cosmos, gathered above and differentiated below, as the Oracle says all things are but intelligibly. Higher cosmoi contain lower ones by embrace and majesty, and beget more orders. Zeus produces like-formed chains by his own property and hetero-named gods such as Dionysus by causes pre-contained in him; the Chaldaean sevenfold Demiurge, each twice-beyond, is synonymous with the whole. He proposes a double partition of every producer: by depth (synonymous chains such as the first and second Zeus) and by breadth (unlike forms, such as the Apolloniac fount within the heliac). He ends by admitting he seems to have returned to the opening question about existence and cause.","ideas":["The higher cosmos contains the lower by embrace, not by having more members","Successor kings proceed whole from whole fathers; other offspring take the father by parts","Partition by depth yields synonymous chains, partition by breadth hetero-named ones","Intellect produces an aphrodisian intellect by the beautiful, an intellect of Dike by the just"],"terms":[{"t":"depth and breadth","d":"The vertical descent of a like-formed chain versus the spread of unlike forms contained in a producer."},{"t":"twice-beyond (dis epekeina)","d":"Chaldaean title of the whole Demiurge, who contains all that the first contains."}]},{"i":421,"j":429,"loc":"Chapter 96, pp. 293–303 (first part)","title":"Cause as existence inclined toward otherness","summary":"Damascius criticizes the ancient view that the producer holds the causes of its offspring as something besides its existence: that cause would itself need a cause, and in-potency is in fact a part of a thing's existence. He suggests that cause is existence inclined toward otherness, then refines this into a threefold existence (like-parted, unlike-parted and by breadth), all products being an unfolding of the producer's gathering. He discusses whole and partial founts, homonymy between the whole and partial Zeus, Iamblichus's partial fount of the Implacables, and concludes that all the founts are gathered in the fount of founts and drunk down in the summit of the intelligible. Outer multitudes grow from inner ones; substances, lives and intellects proceed as elements, parts and numbers; and to be differentiated from the priors is to be begotten from them.","ideas":["Products unfold the producer's gathering as numbers step forth from the monad","Whole founts send forth fountal chains, partial founts archons and archangels","Substances proceed as elements, lives as parts, intellects as a number of forms","Differentiation sets each thing in an existence it had before only within the whole"],"terms":[{"t":"existence (hyparxis)","d":"A thing's own proper being, here shown to contain also what it causes."},{"t":"fount (pēgē)","d":"Chaldaean term for a source-god from which a chain of gods flows."}]},{"i":430,"j":440,"loc":"Chapters 96 (end)–97, pp. 293–306","title":"Individuals, existences, and the many intellects, lives, substances","summary":"Damascius asks whether individuals are gathered in their causes. He first suggests they arise from moving causes acting outwardly, as artefacts do, but then, persuaded that the unmoved first principles cause everything, says one cause holds all recurring individuals, as the sun's light stays common yet is portioned to each eye. He adds that cause, existence and participation (Athena in Zeus, Athena herself, Athena in the Kore) are all existences, and that like-formed procession is defined by proper existences rather than common genera, and is elder than unlike-formed, since each thing proceeds remaining. Chapter 97 asks whether there are many intellects, souls, lives and substances; inner multitudes generate outer ones, so the Demiurge yields Apollonian, Areic and Athenaic intellects, lives proceed like self-complete parts, and substances all but flee back into the undifferentiated.","ideas":["One cause gathers all individuals as the sun's light serves every eye","Every Aphrodite is like-formed to Aphrodite; Eros from Aphrodite is unlike-formed","Like-formed procession is elder, since each thing proceeds remaining","Intellects are circumscribed, lives part-like, substances nearly undifferentiated"],"terms":[{"t":"proceeding while remaining","d":"The principle that a product keeps sameness with its cause even as it goes forth."}]},{"i":441,"j":450,"loc":"Chapters 98–99, pp. 306–312","title":"The simply many and the unparticipated intelligible","summary":"Damascius asks whether the simply many, the undifferent multitude in the intelligible, produce an outer multitude. He argues that since they lack all difference, quantity and quality, they make no proper procession, yet everything down to individuals is begotten from there, as Orpheus's Erikepaios bears the seed of the gods. The intelligible god remains in his hypercosmic watchtower as fount of founts; the Parmenidean triad of One Being, Whole and Parts, and Unlimited Multitude is our analogy from below, since the Father's intellect commanded that all be cut into three. Chapter 99 adds that every cosmos ends into its successor, that the intelligible is wholly unparticipated and sits upon the crown of all, and cites Iamblichus: the intelligible is frozen fast about the One, and its triad is not three monads.","ideas":["The unified multitude is united, not blended, and so does not proceed outward","The intelligible triad is projected upward by analogy with things below","No cosmos descends into the one after it; the intelligible is unparticipated","Before the unified are the many as an unlimited-begetting henad"],"terms":[{"t":"Erikepaios","d":"Orphic god bearing the seed of the gods, from whom their whole generation proceeds."},{"t":"unparticipated (amethekton)","d":"Not shared by or ridden upon by any lower thing."}]},{"i":451,"j":461,"loc":"Chapters 100–101, pp. 312–319","title":"Many self-complete souls, not mere illuminations","summary":"Damascius takes up an ascent from sensible evidence to ask whether after each monad (soul, intellect, life, substance) there are many self-complete members, or only many illuminations from the one, as philosophers before Iamblichus held of the henads. He distinguishes illumination suspended from its source from illumination grown into its recipient, and holds that self-complete subsistence is better than either, so it must belong to higher ranks if it belongs to lower. Because vice and ignorance cannot be affections of the whole soul, human souls are self-complete and self-determining; each ensouled body has its own soul, and the same holds of intellects. Chapter 101 extends the method to life, substance, the unified and the One, and rehearses the proof of other-moved, self-moved and unmoved, the last threefold as substance, life and intellect.","ideas":["Illumination is either suspended from its giver or grown into its receiver","What belongs to the worse belongs still more to the better","Vice and ignorance show partial souls cannot be outgrowths of the whole soul","Unmoved substance is supra-eternal, intellect eternal, life Aion itself"],"terms":[{"t":"self-complete (authypostatos)","d":"Subsisting on its own and seated upon itself, not as another's activity."},{"t":"self-moved","d":"Soul, which begins change in itself and in bodies moved by others."}]},{"i":462,"j":475,"loc":"Chapters 102–103, pp. 319–327","title":"Ascent from appearances to many souls and intellects","summary":"Damascius argues that differing apparent self-motions are evidences of differing true self-moved souls, as in the Phaedrus: the ensouled bodies of Pythagoras, Kylon, Plato and Kleon each have their own soul, which uses its body as an organ or vehicle. He then ascends from the apparent unmoved, the uniform heavenly revolutions, which Plato's Laws makes an image of intellect, to the true unmoved, distinguishing the generated unchanging from the eternal. Since souls differ in form, there are many unmoved intellects, heliac, selenian, Kronian. Chapter 103 shows that this method leads to participated intellects co-ranked with souls, explains that gods are called encosmic or enmattered because henads project their properties onto their vehicles, and grants there are unparticipated multitudes too, though not every appearance warrants ascent to an unparticipated counterpart.","ideas":["Each image differing in form is evidence of its own paradigm","The soul uses its ensouled body as an organ, called its vehicle","Circular heavenly motion is an apparent unmoved imaging intellect","Gods are named encosmic from their own properties, not from what is lower"],"terms":[{"t":"vehicle (ochēma)","d":"The organ ridden by a soul or intellect, co-ranked with it by kinship."},{"t":"apparent unmoved","d":"Motion that keeps always the same manner, an image of the truly unmoved."}]},{"i":476,"j":488,"loc":"Chapters 104–105, pp. 327–335","title":"The unified refuses procession: the hypercosmic abyss","summary":"Damascius argues that an unparticipated multitude precedes the participated one, with a supra-eidetic life before intellect and unparticipated henads before the participated gods, and asks whether the unified likewise yields a double multitude. Of the One he denies any procession at all, since it is without difference and not even anything. In Chapter 105 he concludes that the unified also refuses all differentiation: it has drunk down all things into one union, as the One into one simplicity. With Iamblichus he urges one great intuition that makes the circumference a centre; because we cannot achieve it, we split the unified in two and call it a hidden cosmos and birth-pang of all births. Fearing a titanic rending of our conceptions, we settle on the triad, purifying it by ascending from substance, life and intellect taken before their separation.","ideas":["A middle term between the simply unparticipated and the participated is an unparticipated multitude","The One admits no procession of any kind, not even by analogy","The intelligible abyss is one common birth-pang of all births","Calling the intelligible triadic is a fall we accept to rest our conceptions"],"terms":[{"t":"hypercosmic abyss","d":"The intelligible as hidden cosmos containing all cosmoi undifferentiatedly."},{"t":"birth-pang (ōdis)","d":"Image for the undifferentiated pre-containing of all that will be born."}]},{"i":489,"j":503,"loc":"Chapters 106–108, pp. 335–341","title":"As-it-were procession; the simply One and otherness","summary":"Damascius returns to his thesis that the intelligible makes no outward procession since it does not even proceed inwardly; its multitude is only multitude, of the unlimited and chaos-like kind, so we must call it as-it-were intellect and as-it-were procession. Chapter 107 holds that monad and cosmos are the same There, and that the differentiation Parmenides tried refuted itself, as dividing gold yields only gold: Plato set out a false hypothesis to show the unified's indivisibility. The same intelligible both rides upon all and is exalted above all. Chapter 108 tests the hypothesis of a simply One leading the henads, separated from substance by the middle otherness, with its own unparticipated multitude. Praying that some Mercy steer the argument, Damascius sides with Plato's two columns of One and Substance and asks where such a One could be placed.","ideas":["Inner and outer multitude are the same in the intelligible","Parmenides' division of the unified only confirmed its union","The intelligible is participated and unparticipated before that division","A simply One before the henads would multiply gods before otherness"],"terms":[{"t":"as-it-were (hoion)","d":"Qualifier for names like intellect or form applied to what is beyond them."},{"t":"otherness (heterotēs)","d":"The first differentiation, which separates the one from substance."}]},{"i":504,"j":520,"loc":"Chapters 109–111, pp. 341–349","title":"Grades of the supersubstantial; the Chaldaean triads examined","summary":"Damascius argues that in every rank a unitary and a substantial member are particulars, while the simply is their both-together, so all things are wound off from the intelligible; he ranks the senses of supersubstantial, with the One most properly so. In Chapter 110 he dares to say that from the simply One grows only an unparticipated multitude, the intelligible race of the gods, whose triad indicates rather than divides it. Chapter 111 surveys the theologians: Chaldaean three triads, Orpheus from Chronos to Phanes, Plato's three conclusions. He criticizes the newer philosophers' reading of each triad as two henads and a substantial paternal Intellect, since theologians give generations of gods, not vehicles, and rejects a three-henad alternative as scattering the triads. He proposes instead One as Father, many as Power, and the unified as paternal Intellect.","ideas":["The simply is the both-together of unitary and substantial particulars","The intelligible yields only an unparticipated generation of gods","Theologians hand down generations of the gods themselves, not their vehicles","Father, Power, paternal Intellect answer to one, many, and unified"],"terms":[{"t":"supersubstantial (hyperousios)","d":"Above substance; said in graded senses, most properly of the One."},{"t":"paternal Intellect","d":"Chaldaean third member of the intelligible triad, here identified with the unified."}]},{"i":521,"j":536,"loc":"Chapters 112–114, pp. 349–357","title":"Ordering the cosmoi; substance, life and intellect simply","summary":"Damascius notes the difficulty of producing three triads from a simply one and many, and considers an alternative ordering in which the iynges hold the intelligible, the connectives and teletarchs the intelligible-and-intellectual, and the world-leaders the intellectual, with time an image of Aion. In Chapter 113 he declines to counterfeit the ancestral hypothesis endorsed by Orpheus, the Oracles and Iamblichus: the intelligible is the unified abyss containing simply substance, while Plato's really being substance, seen by the soul's steersman in the Phaedrus, belongs to the summit of life. The undifferentiated is substance, the being-differentiated a flowing and seething called life, the differentiated intellect. Chapter 114 adds that each holds all three by analogy, and that to be substance simply is just to be unified.","ideas":["The connective order fits life because life holds together what is differentiated","Joining determined substance to the intelligible is not joining linen to linen","The summit of life wishes to be substance, as its completion wishes to be intellect","Where one stands so toward being, there the proper kind subsists simply"],"terms":[{"t":"iynges, connectives, teletarchs","d":"Chaldaean orders of gods assigned to successive intelligible-and-intellectual ranks."},{"t":"being-differentiated","d":"The middle state between undifferentiated and differentiated, proper to life."}]},{"i":537,"j":545,"loc":"Chapters 115–116, pp. 357–360","title":"Substance before both, life with both, intellect out of both","summary":"Damascius tests the philosophers' view that in the intelligible one leads and being follows: if meant as rider and ridden, that is already the differentiation proper to intellect, which reverts on itself through otherness. So substance is before one and being, life is with both, and intellect is out of both; the first life holds the eldest differentiation. Chapter 116 climbs a ladder from the soulless form, which nature warms into seething, through plant, animal and the self-moved soul, to the eidetic triad of intellect, and above it a life poured forth beyond reversion and a substance compacted in the undifferentiated. We name these nameless principles from their eidetic parts, so must take the names as indications, by analogy, of a nature surmised from afar.","ideas":["Reversion of intellect into itself presupposes otherness","The animal is defined as a substance living and knowing","Life in intellect is only as-it-were life; simply life is supra-eidetic","The Animal-itself is ranked next after life as the first paradigm"],"terms":[{"t":"reversion (epistrophē)","d":"Turning back toward oneself or one's cause, proper to intellect."},{"t":"supra-eidetic","d":"Above form, as simply life and simply substance are."}]},{"i":546,"j":554,"loc":"Chapter 117, pp. 360–366","title":"No number in the intelligible: one, many, all","summary":"Damascius denies any number in the intelligible and reads the hymned ennead as only the all-complete perfection of its triad. With Iamblichus he says the triad is not three monads, nor even a form, but the one of an undifferentiated triad. Monad, undetermined dyad and triad are not arithmetical: the One is Father, its undetermined power is the many, and the Intellect of the Father is the all, the many determined by reversion. He offers the image of centre, flowing out, and circumference forming a sphere, and urges us not to count the intelligible on our fingers but to open the soul's one greatest eye. He then shows how the whole intelligible triad can be seen in each monad without dividing it, so that the simply One is called untriaded and unmonaded.","ideas":["Arithmetical terms serve as symbols of the numberless","The many stand between the one and the all","The intelligible is one many all, a single nature traversed three ways","Each monad can show the whole triad within itself while staying unified"],"terms":[{"t":"ennead","d":"Ninefold reckoning of the intelligible, read as its triad's all-completeness."},{"t":"undetermined dyad","d":"The second principle, signifying the One's generative cause rather than two units."}]},{"i":555,"j":568,"loc":"Chapters 118–119, pp. 366–372","title":"Human speech for the numberless; dividing the triads","summary":"Damascius concedes that the three principles should no longer be called three, but argues that just as the gods speak to Egyptians, Syrians and Greeks in their own tongues, they teach men in human dialect, words and intuitions alike. Our grasp is a lightning-flash about the eyes, and the discourse rightly disparages itself; the ennead is the triad mirrored like a rainbow from the single-coloured sun, and Socrates in the Philebus showed the triad in the One's forecourt. Chapter 119 asks how to divide the three triads without scattering them, and answers that each monad is itself tripled, as the Chaldaean orders are: the Father is father, power and intellect. The One snatches all into its unspeakable union, so no seeming triad is truly a triad, since every determination is drunk down in the One.","ideas":["The gods adapt their teaching to human language and thought","The ennead is the triad imaged as a rainbow from one sun","Each monad, not each triad, must be divided into three","The unified alone gives a clear showing of the triad"],"terms":[{"t":"pre-appearance, pre-cause, pre-analogy","d":"How the triad and its kin are said to be in what is above the unified."}]},{"i":569,"j":576,"loc":"Chapter 120, pp. 372–375","title":"Existence, power and activity: the Father beyond being","summary":"Damascius takes up the Oracles' names for the three principles, existence, power and activity, which Iamblichus also uses, alongside the Pythagorean monad, dyad and triad and Plato's limit, unlimited and mixed. He asks whether substance and existence differ, reports the philosophers' view that existence belongs to gods and substance to what hangs from them, but says he sees no such difference. He argues that the Father, as cause of being, is not himself being, and is not even in potency, having snatched himself away from aptitude toward substance; the Power is still in potency, and the third principle, taking acting in addition, becomes substance. He concludes that existence differs from substance as being alone differs from being taken together with power and activity.","ideas":["Different schools name the three principles with different triads","To be and to exist are said of the same thing","The first is as it were powerless and actless, better than power and activity","Existence is an element of substance, used to indicate the simplest principle"],"terms":[{"t":"existence, power, activity (hyparxis, dynamis, energeia)","d":"The Oracles' triad for Father, Power and paternal Intellect."}]},{"i":577,"j":590,"loc":"Chapters 121–122, pp. 375–380","title":"Hyparxis as foundation; the threefold unified","summary":"Damascius explains hyparxis, by its name, as a foundation or floor laid beforehand, the simplicity before all composition: cause of all substance but not yet substance, so that one, existence and father coincide. Power is the One's outstretching and pouring, still one but turned toward chaos; the paternal Intellect is the unified, turned back toward the Father by the eldest reversion, which is a union rather than a reversion. Chapter 122 divides the unified as Plato did: One-Being at the summit, Whole and Parts in the middle, Unlimited Multitude, many before all number, at the end, which answers to intellect. He matches these to the uncarved once-beyond intellect, the both-ways-shining Hecate and the twice-beyond, divides the fathers and powers accordingly, and closes his account of the Chaldaean triads without prying into their rites.","ideas":["The pre- in hyparxis marks a principle laid beneath all being","Power wishes to be only a pouring and unlimitedness of the One","The unified's summit is mastered by the one, its end by being","The third intelligible member is hymned as intelligible intellect"],"terms":[{"t":"hyparxis","d":"Existence, explained as a foundation pre-laid for all that is built upon it."},{"t":"father of the fathers","d":"The first of the three fathers when the intelligible Father is divided threefold."}]},{"i":591,"j":611,"loc":"Chapters 123–125, pp. 380–386","title":"Greek and barbarian theogonies of the first principles","summary":"Damascius surveys theologies of the intelligible to deepen reverence for its union. The Orphic Rhapsodies set Time for the One, Aether and Chaos for the two, and the Egg, from which Phanes leaps, for being; the theology of Hieronymus and Hellanicus begins from water and earth, leaves the one before them unspoken, and makes the winged serpent Unaging Time, called Heracles, with Necessity or Adrasteia, then the Egg and the first-born god called Zeus and Pan. From Eudemus he reports Orpheus beginning from Night, Homer's Night feared by Zeus, Hesiod's Chaos, Earth, Tartaros and Eros, Acusilaus, Epimenides and Pherecydes. Among barbarians he gives the Babylonians (Tauthe, Apason, Moymis, Belos), the Magi (Oromasdes and Areimanios), the Sidonians, Mochos's Phoenicians, and the Egyptians' Unknown Darkness and three Kamephis, interpreting each against his triads.","ideas":["Many theologies pass the first principle over in silence","Each tradition is mapped onto the One, the two principles and the intelligible triad","The Egg recurs as the unified or intelligible intellect","The Egyptians divide into many gods what subsists by union"],"terms":[{"t":"Phanes / Protogonos","d":"Orphic first-born god springing from the egg, read as the intelligible intellect."},{"t":"Eudemus","d":"Peripatetic whose history of theologies Damascius draws on."}]},{"i":612,"j":624,"loc":"Chapter 126, pp. 386–390","title":"Participation: the unfinished final chapter","summary":"Damascius begins a treatment of whether the first principles and the intelligible are participated. He defines participating as having something second, from another, distinguishing the giver from the given, as the sun is participated through the light it gives the eyes. He states the objections of Protagoras and Lycophron, who would isolate everything so that the beautiful is only beautiful, together with arguments that participation is neither juxtaposition, union nor blending. He replies that such a view destroys science and even itself, and that experience shows participation everywhere: bodies warmed, souls becoming Pythagorean or Platonic, wood shaped into a throne. Forms must participate one another, motion standing and rest moving, when the text breaks off mid-sentence; a note records the manuscripts passing straight into his Parmenides commentary.","ideas":["Participation is having something after and from another","Isolating every form abolishes predication and refutes itself","Mutual participation of forms: the beautiful and the just share each other","The treatise ends inside an unfinished sentence"],"terms":[{"t":"participation (methexis)","d":"Having a property from another as its source rather than as one's own existence."}]}]});